From 24698c7cfa0da76844981f1de5d5878537022b3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: operator <225459632+operator@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 09:24:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ricursive: shared rounds of 64 rows, one drafter pass a
 round, its pass timed and priced, absorbs batched

Several streams' decode stalled: a shared round held 16 rows over every stream (round_rows), so 8 streams verified
at most one draft each and 16 none, though a GB10 window of 16 rows costs 75 ms against 59 for one. Now:
- a round takes up to 64 rows over its streams (tree_rows; a stream's window stays 16), its widths timed at load
  (24-64 rows), so the depth rule prices shared rounds by what they cost, not the widest window scaled;
- the drafter holds every stream of a round in one pass (hold_streams 16: the lane core prices a batched head as one);
- the drafter times that pass at load by streams and depth (1-16 streams: 14.6 / 22.1 / 54.6 ms at depth 15 on
  four GB10s) and reports hold_costs; the lane core's shared budget (rule.budgets) and headMs price a timed pass by
  its streams and deepest chain instead of step_ms times steps (no other family reports hold_costs);
- a round's verified rows go to the drafter in one plan word and one pass (draft_absorbs): each stream's taps side
  by side, one fc rank sum and one K/V matmul a layer, not two collectives and eleven GEMMs a stream.
Replies are unchanged (greedy goldens byte-identical). Four GB10s, 16 x 128K, SparkMan code decode at 1/2/4/8/16
streams 70.1/106.4/125.7/119.8/148.9 -> 69.6/107.4/153.3/194.0/228.6 tok/s; prose (T 0.7) 26.9/47.9/70.7/90.1/170.0 ->
26.2/46.6/74.8/100.9/146.3.
